If '' is used with the echo command, then the variable in it wont be expanded (wont be opened), while using "" expands it. '' takes everything in it literally, like $name will be printed as $name.

bash -n script.sh is used for a dry run, to know if the script you're trying is correct or not, without actually executing the commands

To make sure that a command only runs if the previous command was successful in a script, then put && at the end of the first line, like echo "no1" && in the first line, and echo "no2" in the second line, now for some reason if the first command isnt able to run, the second wont run either, and if you put || at the end of the second line, and add a third line, then if the previous lines were unable to run, then the thrid line will run, so if line 1 and 2 fail, line 3 will run

